            "lineHeight": 1.4,
            "indent": 0,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Find where the perpendicular through the origin meets the second line, then calculate its distance from the origin.",
                "marks": []
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      "worked": "Let $O=(0,0)$. The shortest segment is perpendicular to both lines. The perpendicular through $O$ has equation $y=-\\frac12x$.\n\nAt its intersection $P$ with $y=2x+\\frac52$:\n\n$$\\begin{align*}-\\frac12x&=2x+\\frac52\\\\-x&=4x+5\\\\-5x&=5\\\\x&=-1\\\\y&=-\\frac12(-1)\\\\y&=\\frac12\\end{align*}$$\n\nThus $P=(-1,\\frac12)$. The shortest distance is\n\n$$\\begin{align*}OP&=\\sqrt{(-1-0)^2+\\left(\\frac12-0\\right)^2}\\\\OP&=\\sqrt{\\frac54}\\\\OP&=\\frac{\\sqrt5}{2}.\\end{align*}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
